From f622c9dfeb060e4404b8e26f3b179ac282b81ab3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zebulan Stanphill <zebulanstanphill@protonmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 21:14:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] UWP: Build Ring Racers as a static library for the UWP
 launcher

Adds SRB2_CONFIG_UWP (and the ninja-x64_windows_uwp_vcpkg-release preset),
which builds the game as a static library to be linked into the UWP
launcher from ringracers-uwp. Based on worleydl's uwp-compat branch of
RingRacers-dev, rebased onto v2.4.

- Pull the SDL2 headers and libuwp from worleydl/uwp-dep instead of vcpkg
- Compile for the Xbox One's Jaguar CPU (btver2), whose instructions
  Xbox Series X|S CPUs also support
- Render through OpenGL ES 2 (ANGLE on Direct3D 11): support OpenGL ES 2
  contexts in the GL2 RHI backend, request one, and render at the size of
  ANGLE's surface
- Draw Legacy GL with Direct3D 11, since it needs desktop OpenGL, which
  ANGLE doesn't have, and name it "VIBErant Direct3D 11": a
  driver for its hardware renderer interface (hardware/r_d3d11) that
  follows r_opengl.c's OpenGL state call for call, with its GLSL shaders
  ported to HLSL and compiled by fxc at build time. Each frame is copied
  to a texture shared with ANGLE's device and drawn to the window through
  OpenGL ES a frame later, or read back if ANGLE can't open the texture.
  Its startup, presentation path, and errors are logged, and the perf
  summary adds Legacy GL's renderer timings and the driver's draws, state
  changes, texture uploads, CPU time, presentation timings, and GPU time
- Leave precipitation and things with RF_HIDEINSKYBOX out of Legacy GL's
  skybox view, as the software renderer does
- Port SRB2's palette rendering to Legacy GL (gr_paletterendering, on by
  default, in Legacy GL's options): textures are made in the palette
  lump's colors, shaders turn texels back into palette indices with a
  64x64x64 lookup table and light them through each colormap's light
  table, and the finished frame is turned into the screen palette, which
  brings palette flashes and color correction without remaking textures
- Invert the screen for Encore level starts in Legacy GL instead of
  drawing an opaque white screen, while keeping the screen alpha at 1
- Match the RHI screen wipes in Legacy GL: use raw fade-mask bytes for
  Mega Drive-style fades to black and white, inversion and crossfades;
  swap the captured screens and reverse the mask for reversed fades,
  and shift rows with Encore's bottom-origin wiggle while clamping
  screen sampling to the captured image. Keep the two-pass wipe as a
  fallback if the full wipe's resources are unavailable
- Show each Legacy GL wipe frame immediately instead of presenting the
  previous frame, so Encore's inversion circle reaches the screen edges
  before presenting stops; keep normal shared presentation a frame late
- Refresh the pre-level title card's clock after loading on UWP, so its
  animation plays in full; retain upstream's one step per frame and HUD
  timer clamp. This does not affect game logic
- Log local and non-local video memory budgets, usage and reservations at
  Legacy GL startup and in perf summaries, alongside tracked texture,
  model/static buffer, ring buffer and approximate screen allocations.
  Rate-limit immediate GPU-frame and copy-timeout warnings, including
  local usage and budget and counts of suppressed warnings
- Request a Legacy GL texture-cache flush when local video memory usage
  exceeds its budget, consuming it before frame drawing or wipe capture
  in D_Display, at most once every five seconds. Retain model buffers
- Make fewer GL calls, which are expensive through ANGLE on the Xbox's
  CPU: skip state changes and binds in the GL2 RHI backend that wouldn't
  change anything, cache uniform locations, keep framebuffers between
  frames, keep colormap and lighttable textures between frames
  (reuploading them only when they change), and only upload the palette
  when it changes
- Spend less time on work the game doesn't need, which costs a lot on
  the Xbox's CPU: clear only the columns of a new visplane that can be on
  screen, inline FixedMul, don't sleep after a frame skipped to catch up
  on game logic as if it had been drawn, remember which subsector recent
  points are in, skip sprite sorting nodes that share no column with the
  sprite without reading what they point to, keep a set of the pointers
  that have Lua userdata so freeing a block doesn't ask Lua, store
  single-channel textures as GL_RED (EXT_texture_rg), which ANGLE doesn't
  convert to RGBA on every upload the way it does GL_LUMINANCE, size the
  thread pool from processors explicitly allocated to the game (falling
  back to the upstream hardware count when Xbox doesn't identify them),
  and record interpolated mobjs' slots for direct removal from the list
- Rotate tilted software views by stepping along source rows, instead
  of rebuilding and reading a full-screen 4-byte-per-pixel map. Preserve
  the original float stepping, clipping, zoom, and mirrored sampling;
  share large views across the software rendering workers. Cache by
  viewport dimensions and source pitch, and fill odd-height center rows
- Cut repeated setup in the software renderer: leave unused thick-side
  slots alone, inline texture translation lookup, reuse each masked
  column's clipping limits, pass read-only column data directly to
  drawers, and calculate each FOF plane's bounds once during node sorting
- Inline release-build mobj reference updates and removal checks while
  preserving their order around collision callbacks and all PARANOIA
  diagnostics
- Fix P_SetMobjState and P_SetPlayerMobjState returning early without
  counting their call as finished when a state removes its mobj, after
  which every state change cleared a table of every state (about 40 KB).
  Each nested call now uses a table for its depth, and erases only the
  states it recorded
- Read game data and save files from E:\ringracers
- Ask at launch whether to check the main asset files, since that reads
  all of them from the USB drive, or to quit (also B, which used to act
  like A). Skipping uses the expected MD5s instead
  of hashing the files, hashes only the names of the music and sound
  files (which have no expected MD5), and doesn't read every zip entry's
  local header to check the layout and find where its data starts; that's
  found the first time the entry is read instead
- Use the libuwp display size and refresh rate instead of SDL's
- Force WASAPI audio
- Write the log to E:\ringracers\latest-log.txt, since the working
  directory is the read-only package folder
- Create the save folder before checking that the config is writable
- Log the OpenGL strings, surface size, shader compiles, and each step
  of the first frames, to diagnose startup on Xbox
- Add UWP-only, bounded frame-position/presentation and fade/palette traces
  to diagnose race animation stutter and title-screen brightness, including
  main-view snapshots, slot source draw IDs, wipes and title-map lighting
- Log the track title animation's frame count and elapsed time, with
  drawing/presentation timings, to diagnose slow or uneven animation
- Log how long each loading step and asset file takes and how much of
  that was spent logging, the thread pool size, the process memory
  budget, and every 5 seconds a summary of the perfstats frame and tic timings, the parts of
  I_FinishUpdate, and counts of draws, texture uploads, and resources
  created
- Sample the main thread about 50 times a second while a level is
  played, and write how many samples landed at each address and with each
  call stack (found from each module's unwind data), by what the game was
  doing, to E:\ringracers\uwp-profile.txt. The perf summary also logs how
  long the samples paused the main thread, which processors it ran on, the
  level's mobjs, P_CheckPosition calls and Lua mobj hooks per tic, and how
  often R_PointInSubsector's cache answered, and startup logs the CPU sets
  Windows lists for the game
- Log crashes and their call stacks from a vectored exception handler,
  since the app runtime, not the C runtime, starts the main thread, so the
  signal handler never sees an access violation there
- Skip the DbgHelp minidump handler and cpptrace, which can't work from
  the app container
- Show only the error in the crash dialog, which the Xbox cuts off,
  followed by which drives the app can see and whether each has a
  ringracers folder, and save it to E:\ringracers\error.txt

